{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-334148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′}-1)^{4}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x{y}^{5}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{2}$\n- $x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2)-y=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=({y}^{′}-1)^{4}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{4}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y.\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2),\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$\n- $x{y}^{4}-xy{y}^{′}-{y}^{2}=0$\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx{y}^{3}-x{y}^{′}-y=0.\n$$\n\nAgain, this equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y+2=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}$\n- ${y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′})$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-y=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}-2.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={y}^{′}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-321228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{3}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}-y=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-321228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{2}+2xy=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-321228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-321228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-321228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-321228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})$\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+{2}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- ${y}^{2}=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{2}{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. It cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,{y}^{′}>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p).\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p))}^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+\\frac{1}{p}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+\\frac{1}{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c,\n$$\n\nwith the restriction $c>0.$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p),$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c).\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2c)"] ["math" "y=-cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c)"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c)x+c"] ["math" "y=cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c)"] ["math" "y=cx+c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{e}^{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{e}^{p}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+{e}^{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+{e}^{p},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+{e}^{c}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=cx+c"] ["math" "y=cx+{e}^{2c}"] ["math" "y=cx+{e}^{c}"] ["math" "y={c}^{2}x+{e}^{c}"] ["math" "y={c}^{3}x+{e}^{c}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=cx+{e}^{c}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-325651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-325651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+{p}^{2},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+{c}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-325651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-325651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-325651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=cx+2c"] ["math" "y=cx+c"] ["math" "y=cx+{c}^{2}"] ["math" "y={c}^{2}x+2c"] ["math" "y={c}^{2}x+c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-325651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=cx+{c}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-7{y}^{′}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-7{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-7p.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(xp-7p)}^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-7{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp-7{p}^{′},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx-7c.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=cx+7"] ["math" "y=cx+7{c}^{2}"] ["math" "y=cx-7c"] ["math" "y={c}^{2}x+7c"] ["math" "y={c}^{2}x-7c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=cx-7c"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{2}+4({y}^{′})^{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{2}+4({y}^{′})^{3},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-{p}^{2}+4{p}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp-{p}^{2}+4{p}^{3})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-2p{p}^{′}+12{p}^{2}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-2p+12{p}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp-{p}^{2}+4{p}^{3},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx-{c}^{2}+4{c}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=cx+4{c}^{3}"] ["math" "y=cx-{c}^{2}"] ["math" "y=cx-{c}^{2}+4{c}^{3}"] ["math" "y={c}^{2}x-{c}^{2}+4{c}^{3}"] ["math" "y={c}^{3}x-{c}^{2}+4{c}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=cx-{c}^{2}+4{c}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+p-3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+p-3)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+1=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+p-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+p-3 \\\\  & =(-1)p+p-3 \\\\  & =-p+p-3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-3"] ["math" "y=-3x"] ["math" "y=1-3x"] ["math" "y=3x+1"] ["math" "y=3{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+2p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{p}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2p \\\\ y=-{p}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "y=-{x}^{2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-321239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-321239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{e}^{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{p}^{′}{e}^{p} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+{e}^{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+{e}^{p}=0.$ Then, \n\n$$\nx=-{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{e}^{p}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{e}^{p} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{p})p+{e}^{p} \\\\  & =(1-p){e}^{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-{e}^{p} \\\\ y=(1-p){e}^{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)\n$$\n\nSince $x=-{e}^{p}$ and ${e}^{p}>0,$ we must have $x<0.$ Thus, $p=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)$ is valid only for $x<0.$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(1-p){e}^{p} \\\\  & =(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)){e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)} \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1),\\,x<0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-321239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-321239" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-321239" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1,\\,x<0"] ["math" "y=x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1),\\,x<0"] ["math" "y=x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1),\\,x>0"] ["math" "y=x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-1),\\,x>0"] ["math" "y={x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1),\\,x<0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-321239" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1),\\,x<0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-{p}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp-{p}^{4})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-4{p}^{3}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-4{p}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x-4{p}^{3}=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=4{p}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp-{p}^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =(4{p}^{3})p-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =4{p}^{4}-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4{p}^{3} \\\\ y=3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np={(\\frac{x}{4})}^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{({(\\frac{x}{4})}^{1/3})}^{4} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}"] ["math" "y=-3{(\\frac{x}{9})}^{4/3}"] ["math" "y=-3{x}^{4/3}"] ["math" "y=3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+2({y}^{′})^{2}+3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+2({y}^{′})^{2}+3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+2{p}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+2{p}^{2}+3)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+4p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+4p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+4p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-4p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+2{p}^{2}+3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =(-4p)p+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-4{p}^{2}+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4p \\\\ y=-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$ \n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{(-\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0740448e9b720813dc79f7eb466eb59030d4da806e29825ec90a5d160413409"} {"question" "q-334454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=3+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}"] ["math" "y=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "y=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}"] ["math" "y=3-{x}^{2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "q-334454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/state.json" "file_sha256" "f94ed3a4f8472ac146a53044345018e64a2d4ff230970d144f22e1a5f53ddded"} {"question" "e-21082" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y -xy' -\\ln(y')=0$\n\n- $y^4\\cos(x) +xyy' = y^2$\n\n- $y= xy' + 2y'$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/example-21082.json" "file_sha256" "a9a45a0594e850103cdc0faa8950752984ddc128f63361f0b5ddb80ea329c9c3"} {"question" "e-21082" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = xy' + \\ln(y'),\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\ng(y') = \\ln(y').\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny^3\\cos(x) +xy' = y.\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y = xy' + g(y').$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with \n\n$$\ng(y') = 2y'.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/example-21082.json" "file_sha256" "a9a45a0594e850103cdc0faa8950752984ddc128f63361f0b5ddb80ea329c9c3"} {"question" "e-21413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny = xy' + e^{-y'}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/example-21413.json" "file_sha256" "eef6a205985fa8d443395956656f402c9f807b4a18e5f44f4f39e3ff2781efd4"} {"question" "e-21413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + e^{-y'},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = x p + e^{-p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{e}^{-p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-{e}^{-p}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-{e}^{-p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $p' = 0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np = c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p = c$ into $y = x p + e^{-p},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = cx + e^{-c}\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/example-21413.json" "file_sha256" "eef6a205985fa8d443395956656f402c9f807b4a18e5f44f4f39e3ff2781efd4"} {"question" "e-21083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/example-21083.json" "file_sha256" "bda44fcb5ca2bfdea6c0f2281376f336a5c56ff1e7f034778ed6a4b87be31f4f"} {"question" "e-21083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2 +1,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2+1.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x + 2p = 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx = -2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y = xp + p^2+1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2p \\\\ y=-{p}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p{:}$\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny = 1- \\dfrac{x^2}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054585/example-21083.json" "file_sha256" "bda44fcb5ca2bfdea6c0f2281376f336a5c56ff1e7f034778ed6a4b87be31f4f"}] "usage" {"e-21082" [] "e-21413" [] "q-321228" [] "e-21083" [] "q-321239" [] "q-334398" [] "q-325651" []} "basis" 1804 "retractions" [[17592186217724 :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n1. $y = xy^{′} + (y^{′})^{3}$\n2. $yy^{′} - xy^{3} + 2xy^{2} = \\sin (x)y$\n3. $xy^{′} + e^{y^{′}} - y = 0$"] [17592186217727 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$y = xy^{′} + (y^{′})^{2}$."] [17592186217743 :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$y = xy^{′} + (y^{′})^{2}$."] [17592186217725 :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$y = xy^{′} + e^{y^{′}}$."] [17592186216940 :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n1. $y -xy' -\\ln(y')=0$\n2. $y^4\\cos(x) +xyy' = y^2$\n3. $y= xy' + 2y'$"] [17592186216940 :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y$, we get\n$y = xy' + \\ln(y')$,\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with\n$g(y') = \\ln(y')$.\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y$, we get\n$y^3\\cos(x) +xy' = y$.\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y = xy' + g(y')$.\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with\n$g(y') = 2y'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186216944 :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n$y = xy' + e^{-y'}$"] [17592186216944 :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + e^{-y'}\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'$.\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = x p + e^{-p}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (xp + e^{-p})^{′} \\\\\np &= p + xp^{′} - e^{-p}p^{′} \\\\\n0 &= p^{′}(x - e^{-p})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $p' = 0$. Then, $p$ is constant, say\n$p = c$.\n\nSubstituting $p = c$ into\n\n$$\ny = x p + e^{-p}\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = cx + e^{-c}\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c$."] [17592186216941 :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n$y = xy' + (y')^2+1$."] [17592186216941 :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'$.\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2 +1\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'$.\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2+1\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (xp + p^{2} + 1)^{′} \\\\\np &= p + xp^{′} + 2pp^{′} \\\\\n0 &= p^{′}(x + 2p)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if\n\n$$\nx + 2p = 0\n$$\n\nThen,\n$x = -2p$.\n\nNow, let's substitute this into\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2+1{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= xp + p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= (-2p)p + p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&=-2p^{2} + p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&=-p^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{x =-2p; y =-p^{2} + 1\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p$, we first solve the first equation for $p{:}$\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= - (-\\frac{x}{2})^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny = 1- \\dfrac{x^2}{4}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186216442 17592186216443 17592186216444 17592186216445 17592186216446 17592186216448 17592186216449 17592186216450 17592186216451 17592186216452 17592186216460 17592186216461 17592186216462 17592186216463 17592186216464 17592186216550 17592186216551 17592186216552 17592186216553 17592186216554] "immutable_fields" [[17592186216441 :answer-field/correct] [17592186216441 :answer-field/id] [17592186216441 :answer-field/key] [17592186216441 :answer-field/type] [17592186216447 :answer-field/correct] [17592186216447 :answer-field/id] [17592186216447 :answer-field/key] [17592186216447 :answer-field/type] [17592186216459 :answer-field/correct] [17592186216459 :answer-field/id] [17592186216459 :answer-field/key] [17592186216459 :answer-field/type] [17592186216549 :answer-field/correct] [17592186216549 :answer-field/id] [17592186216549 :answer-field/key] [17592186216549 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
